Actually, let me look at this from a completely different angle. Let me look at the actual crash by examining the code. The fix commit adds a check for `GF_M4ASampleRates[ctx->acfg.base_sr_index]` being 0. This is in `latm_dmx_check_pid` and `latm_dmx_process`.

The `GF_M4ASampleRates` array has 16 entries. `base_sr_index` is 4 bits (0-15). So index 15 is within bounds. But the fix checks for value 0, which happens at indices 13, 14, 15.

Wait, but the description says "Stack-buffer-overflow READ". Let me reconsider. Maybe the crash is a READ because of the `memcpy(output, latm_buffer, latm_frame_size)` in `latm_dmx_process`. If `latm_frame_size` (which is `size`) is larger than 4096, then `memcpy` reads `latm_frame_size` bytes from `latm_buffer` (which is only 4096 bytes) → stack-buffer-overflow READ.

But wait, `gf_bs_read_data` would have already written `size` bytes into `latm_buffer`, overflowing it. So the WRITE would be detected first by ASAN.

Unless... the `gf_bs_read_data` doesn't actually write all `size` bytes because the bitstream is at the end. Let me re-read:

```c
if (bs->position+nbBytes > bs->size) return 0;
```

So if `bs->position + nbBytes > bs->size`, it returns 0 without writing. But `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs` checks `if (gf_bs_available(bs) < size) { return GF_FALSE; }`. So `size <= gf_bs_available(bs)`. And `gf_bs_available(bs)` is `bs->size - bs->position`. So `bs->position + size <= bs->size`. So `gf_bs_read_data` will write all `size` bytes.

So the WRITE happens first. The crash should be a WRITE.

Hmm, but the description says READ. Let me reconsider.

Actually, wait. Let me reconsider the `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs` function more carefully:

```c
static Bool latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs(GF_BitStream *bs, GF_M4ADecSpecInfo *acfg, u32 *frame_size, u8 *buffer, u32 *nb_bytes)
{
    u32 size;
    u64 pos = gf_bs_get_position(bs);
    ...
    size = 0;
    while (1) {
        u32 tmp = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 8);
        size += tmp;
        if (tmp!=255) break;
    }
    if (gf_bs_available(bs) < size) {
        gf_bs_seek(bs, pos-3);
        return GF_FALSE;
    }
    if (nb_bytes) {
        *nb_bytes = (u32) size;
    }
    if (buffer) {
        gf_bs_read_data(bs, (char *) buffer, size);
    }
    ...
}
```

Wait, `size` is a `u32`. The loop reads bytes and adds them to `size`. If the input has many `0xFF` bytes, `size` can grow large. But `gf_bs_available(bs)` limits it. So `size <= gf_bs_available(bs)`. And `gf_bs_read_data` writes `size` bytes into `buffer` (the 4096-byte stack buffer). If `size > 4096`, this is a stack-buffer-overflow WRITE.

So the crash should be a WRITE. But the description says READ.

